Z||000005||bHooper||05-15-2001||06:56 AM||||yep, vacuum leaks can do that.  when i put my car back together, last year, i had glowing manifolds.<P>i'm no authority, but apparently there are three probable causes of glowing manifolds:<BR>1)vacuum leak<BR>2)timeing<BR>3)clogged cat<P>the first is the easiest to fix.<P>best of luck!<P>hoop<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000006||Ken Wittlief||05-15-2001||10:34 AM||||A vacuum leak will make the exhaust gasses very hot because the mixture leans out.<P> Its like a welding tourch. If you light it and only burn the gas with the air around the flame, the flame is white, and not very hot.<P>  as you start opening the oxygen valve on the torch, the flame turns bluer, and gets hotter.<P>  Its the same way with a car engine. when properly mixed the fuel and air produces the max volume of exhaust (and pressure) to move the cylinders - which is right on the edge of being a sooty flame.<P>  but if you add more air for the same amount of fuel, the mixture burns hotter, hot enought to melt the ends off the spark plugs, and to make your exhaust manifold glow red hot. A lean engine runs very hot - and thats what a vacuum leak causes, a lean mixture.||||reg||1
Z||000007||84 2in1||05-15-2001||05:27 PM||||Well it isn't my cat because it has seemed to disappear! Timing may be off. pulled the distributor so I may bee 180* out of time. I'll have to check these things out on my next trip. Thanx-B-||||reg||1
Z||000008||ebhan||05-22-2001||01:28 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by 84 2in1:<BR><B>Well it isn't my cat because it has seemed to disappear! Timing may be off. pulled the distributor so I may bee 180* out of time. I'll have to check these things out on my next trip. Thanx-B-</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>I have some exhaust (manifold) overheating problem driving up-hill at low RPM (glows read). Checked timing, EGR, vacum leaks, replaced catalytic converter, etc -- still too hot in my oppinion!<P>First time I hear an "out-of-sync" distributer might cause overheating problem. My distributer axle-drive appears to be instered 60 degrees off (machanic did this when installing new motor), but the wires are moved correspondingly. Moter runns great-- just too warm an exhaust upon load!<P>Will the moter or computer "know" any difference -- as long as the spark go to the right cylender at the right time (end of compression stroke)? <P><BR>Ebhan -- just had a new 2.8 motor installed in my 88 GT.<P><BR>||||reg||5
Z||000009||ThatRickGuy||05-22-2001||09:24 AM||||I've been running w/ my dist 180 degress off for a few months now(flipped the plug wires to compensate). Going 60 off might be a little odd, but as long as the plug wires were moved to the correct places and the timing was set you should be okay. Check for vacuum leaks, fuel pressure, etc, anything that could cause a lean mixture, if you can't find anything, take it to an emmissions shop, they should be able to put a sniffer on it and tell you if it is indead running lean. if it's not, then I would say replace the cat.||||reg||1
